{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HUfpsw2uG2BdJWbpmAdfdr","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent! Now let me compile a comprehensive summary of the findings:\n\n## Coding Conventions Summary: go-unix-utils cmd/ utilities\n\nBased on my analysis of exemplary utilities in the repo, here's a detailed breakdown of the coding conventions:\n\n### 1. COPYRIGHT HEADER FORMAT\n\nAll main.go and test files use this consistent format:\n\n```\n// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n```\n\nThen followed by a package doc comment describing the implementation:\n\n```go\n// cmd/cat implements srd006-cat: file concatenation with line numbering,\n// blank squeezing, and non-printing display.\npackage main\n```\n\n### 2. FLAG PARSING\n\nThe repo **uses manual flag parsing** (no standard library `flag` or `pflag` packages). Three patterns are used:\n\n**Pattern A: Simple character-based flags (cat, tee)**\n- Manual iteration through flag characters\n- Global `var` declarations for flags\n- `parseFlags()` function manually parses `-xyz` style flags\n- See `/cmd/cat/main.go` lines 19-26 and 55-104\n\n```go\nvar (\n    flagN bool\n    flagB bool\n    flagS bool\n    flagV bool\n    flagE bool\n    flagT bool\n)\n\nfunc parseFlags(args []string) []string {\n    var files []string\n    endOfFlags := false\n    for _, arg := range args {\n        if endOfFlags || arg == \"\" || arg == \"-\" || arg[0] != '-' {\n            files = append(files, arg)\n            continue\n        }\n        if arg == \"--\" {\n            endOfFlags = true\n            continue\n        }\n        for _, c := range arg[1:] {\n            applyFlag(c)\n        }\n    }\n    return files\n}\n```\n\n**Pattern B: Complex long-form flags with values (head, tail, sponge)**\n- Separate `parseLongFlag()` and `parseShortFlags()` functions\n- Options struct to hold parsed values\n- Handles `--flag=value` and `--flag value` patterns\n- See `/cmd/head/main.go` lines 55-250 and `/cmd/sponge/main.go` lines 22-31\n\n```go\nvar appendMode = flag.Bool(\"a\", false, \"append to output file\")\n\ntype options struct {\n    mode   countMode\n    count  int64\n    header headerMode\n}\n\nfunc parseArgs(args []string) (options, []string, error) {\n    opts := options{count: 10}\n    // ... parsing logic\n}\n```\n\n**Pattern C: Signal-based flag handling (tee)**\n- Uses `signal.Ignore()` for SIGPIPE instead of custom handler\n- See `/cmd/tee/main.go` lines 16-20\n\n### 3. MAIN() STRUCTURE & HELPER FUNCTIONS\n\n**Standard main() pattern:**\n1. Call `sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()` first (or `signal.Ignore(syscall.SIGPIPE)`)\n2. Parse flags/args\n3. Process files/stdin\n4. Track exitCode and return via `os.Exit()`\n5. Only call `os.Exit()` if error occurred\n\nExample from `/cmd/cat/main.go` lines 28-53:\n\n```go\nfunc main() {\n    sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()\n    \n    args := parseFlags(os.Args[1:])\n    if len(args) == 0 {\n        args = []string{\"-\"}\n    }\n    \n    exitCode := 0\n    lineNum := 1\n    prevBlank := false\n    for _, name := range args {\n        var err error\n        lineNum, prevBlank, err = catFile(name, lineNum, prevBlank)\n        if err != nil {\n            if errors.Is(err, syscall.EPIPE) {\n                os.Exit(0)\n            }\n            fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, \"cat: %s\\n\", formatErr(err))\n            exitCode = 1\n        }\n    }\n    if exitCode != 0 {\n        os.Exit(exitCode)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Helper functions:**\n- File-specific functions: `catFile()`, `headLines()`, `tailBytes()`\n- Transformation functions: `writeLine()`, `writeTransformed()`\n- Error handling helpers: `formatErr()`\n- All use `io.Reader`/`io.Writer` abstractions where possible\n\n### 4. SIGPIPE HANDLER PATTERN\n\n**Primary pattern (cat, head, tail, sponge):**\n```go\nimport \"github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys\"\n\nfunc main() {\n    sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()\n    // ... rest of main\n}\n```\n\nLocated at `/pkg/sys/sigpipe.go` - uses signal.Notify with buffered channel, calls `os.Exit(0)` on SIGPIPE.\n\n**Alternative pattern (tee):**\n```go\nimport \"os/signal\"\nimport \"syscall\"\n\nfunc main() {\n    signal.Ignore(syscall.SIGPIPE)\n    // ... rest of main\n}\n```\n\nTee ignores SIGPIPE because it writes to files and returns EPIPE errors naturally.\n\n### 5. IMPORT STYLE\n\nOrganized into groups with blank lines:\n\n```go\nimport (\n    // Standard library: alphabetically ordered\n    \"bufio\"\n    \"errors\"\n    \"fmt\"\n    \"io\"\n    \"os\"\n    \"syscall\"\n    \n    // Project imports: single group after blank line\n    \"github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys\"\n)\n```\n\n## Test File Patterns\n\n**Test utility usage (testutils.RunDiffTests):**\n\nLocated at `/pkg/testutils/difftest.go` and `/pkg/testutils/build.go`\n\n**BuildBinary pattern:**\n```go\nfunc TestDiff(t *testing.T) {\n    goBin := testutils.BuildBinary(t, \".\")  // \".\" = current cmd package\n    refBin, err := exec.LookPath(\"gcat\")    // GNU reference binary\n    if err != nil {\n        t.Skip(\"reference binary not found\")\n    }\n    // ... test cases\n}\n```\n\n**DiffTest struct** (from `/pkg/testutils/difftest.go` lines 46-55):\n```go\ntype DiffTest struct {\n    Name          string\n    Args          []string\n    Stdin         []byte\n    Env           []string\n    WorkDir       string\n    ExitCode      int\n    Normalize     []NormalizeFunc\n    ExpectedFiles map[string][]byte  // For file-output verification\n}\n```\n\n**ExpectedFiles usage example** (from `/cmd/uniq/uniq_test.go` lines 110-117):\n```go\n{\n    Name:    \"r1_3_output_file\",\n    Args:    []string{\"dups.txt\", \"out.txt\"},\n    WorkDir: dir,\n    Env:     env,\n    ExpectedFiles: map[string][]byte{\n        \"out.txt\": []byte(\"a\\nb\\na\\n\"),\n    },\n}\ntestutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBin, refBin, tests)\n```\n\nThe testutils framework:\n- Runs same test with both Go and reference binary\n- Compares stdout, stderr, exit code\n- WorkDir used when tests need file I/O (defaults to t.TempDir())\n- Env allows test-specific environment overrides (defaults to LC_ALL=C)\n- ExpectedFiles verifies file creation/modification after test runs\n- Normalize applies cleanup functions (e.g., timestamp normalization)\n\nAll code examples are absolute paths within the worktree:\n- `/cmd/cat/main.go` and `/cmd/cat/cat_test.go`\n- `/cmd/tee/main.go` and `/cmd/tee/tee_test.go`\n- `/cmd/sponge/main.go` and `/cmd/sponge/sponge_test.go`\n- `/cmd/head/main.go` and `/cmd/tail/main.go`\n- `/pkg/sys/sigpipe.go`\n- `/pkg/testutils/difftest.go` and `/pkg/testutils/build.go`"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"417d3d97-6d07-4097-ab1c-960277983888","uuid":"d0197434-9675-4eb4-8f39-9117f3058ca7","timestamp":"2026-05-20T23:55:18.772Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"In the repo at /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5391, I need to understand the coding conventions used in cmd/ utilities. Find a few existing cmd/ main.go files that are good examples (preferably ones that handle file operations like cat, mv, or similar). Show me:\n1. The copyright header format\n2. How they parse flags (standard library flag package vs pflag vs manual)\n3. How they structure main() and helper functions\n4. How they call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()\n5. The import style\n\nAlso show me a test file (cp_test.go pattern) from any cmd/ that uses testutils.RunDiffTests and testutils.BuildBinary, especially one that uses ExpectedFiles or WorkDir.\n\nSearch breadth: medium","agentId":"aa3b6f64222411b5d","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent! Now let me compile a comprehensive summary of the findings:\n\n## Coding Conventions Summary: go-unix-utils cmd/ utilities\n\nBased on my analysis of exemplary utilities in the repo, here's a detailed breakdown of the codin