mpt to stderr without a trailing newline. After normalizing the binary name in the output, both should show \"cp: overwrite 'existing.txt'? \" identically. My Go implementation writes the prompt to stderr the same way.\n\nNow I'm setting up the test file itself and thinking through what error normalizers I'll need—similar to what the uniq and ln tests use, like binary name replacement, lowercasing stderr, removing help text lines, and stripping the \"open \" prefix from errors. I'm also considering whether I need to handle platform-specific differences in errno messages, since macOS and Linux capitalize them differently. After normalizing the binary name, these match too. ✓\n\nNow I'm checking the \"cannot stat\" error—after normalizing both the binary name and the case differences, my output matches GNU cp's. ✓\n\nI'm confident the error messages will align with proper normalizers in place. Now I need to write the test file, but I should be careful to only apply error normalizers to error test cases, not to successful ones.\n\nFor interactive tests though, the stderr includes the prompt with the binary name, so I need to normalize that too. I'm thinking of creating two separate normalizer sets: one for error cases that handles binary name, case, and \"Try\" lines, and another for interactive tests that just normalizes the binary name in the prompt.\n\nDefining the normalizers... Setting up test normalizers...\n\nI'm concerned the regex might be too broad—it could match \"cp\" within other words or command output. Looking at how the uniq test handles this with a similar pattern, I need to be more careful about word boundaries to ensure I'm only matching the actual binary name and not substrings. by `:`. Looking at the actual error message format, my Go binary hardcodes \"cp:\" in the output while the GNU version uses \"gcp:\", so I need a regex that matches the binary name followed by a colon without accidentally catching \"cp\" appearing elsewhere in the error text.\n\nThe test harness runs binaries with their full path as argv[0], so both my implementation and GNU cp would see the full path initially. But GNU cp uses the short name (stripping the directory), whereas I'm hardcoding just \"cp\" in my error messages, which creates a mismatch when comparing outputs. I need to be more precise with the regex pattern. Using word boundaries with `\\bg?cp\\b` would correctly match \"cp\" or \"gcp\" only as complete words, so it wouldn't accidentally match the \"cp\" inside \"cpt\" or other words where it's just a substring. Looking at the error messages from the cp command, the word \"cp\" only appears at the start as the program name, never within the message text itself, so the regex `\\bg?cp\\b` should safely match without false positives. But I need to account for cases where the Go binary path might contain \"cp\" in a directory name like `/tmp/TestDiff_cp123/cp`, which would still produce error messages starting with just `gcp:`.\n\nRefining the regex pattern...\n\nI'm realizing the word boundary approach creates issues—`\\b` would still match \"tcp\" since it treats \"cp\" as a separate word. The safest approach is to stick with the same pattern used elsewhere: `(?:/\\S+/)?g?cp`, accepting that false positives are unlikely in cp's actual error output. I realize Go's regex engine doesn't support lookahead, so I need a different approach—using multiline mode to match the binary name only at the start of each line, followed by a colon, which ensures I'm only replacing the actual command name in error messages and not accidentally matching \"cp\" inside filenames.\n\nLet me verify this works correctly against the test cases and then finalize the test file. I'm also thinking through how the interactive prompt will handle the stdin input when the Go binary reads the response.\n\nWriting test file... I'm setting up test normalization functions to standardize binary names, convert error messages to lowercase, and filter out \"try\" help suggestions. Then I'm defining separate normalization rules for error output versus prompt output, and starting to write test cases that copy files and compare the behavior between the reference and test binaries. Writing test cases... Writing test cases... Adding more test cases... Writing test cases... Writing test cases... Writing test cases... Looking at the existing test patterns, I should stick with short flags rather than testing long flag variants separately since the behavior is what matters. I'm adding a test case to verify that the `-n` flag still creates new files when the destination doesn't exist, since it only prevents overwriting existing ones.\n\nWriting test code...\n\nNow I'm realizing there's a potential issue in my implementation. When handling the `-n` and `-i` flags, I check if the destination file exists first, but I need to be careful about the order of operations—particularly with the `-f` flag, which should remove the destination file before attempting to create it, even if it's read-only.\n\nLet me trace through the `-f -n` scenario with a read-only destination file: the stat succeeds since the file exists, then the `-n` flag prevents the copy from happening, so the read-only status becomes irrelevant. That's actually correct behavior—`-n` takes precedence and blocks the operation entirely.\n\nFor `-f -i` with a read-only file where the user confirms, the flow works as expected: stat finds the file, the interactive prompt gets user approval, then `doCopy` attempts to open it, fails due to read-only permissions, and the `-f` flag triggers a removal and retry that succeeds.\n\nAnd when copying to a writable destination with `-f`, the flag has no practical effect since the file opens normally without needing to be removed first.\n\nBut I'm realizing GNU cp's `-f` behavior might not be exactly what I've been assuming... about read-only files. From the man page:\n\n```\n-f, --force\n    if an existing destination file cannot be opened, remove it and try again\n```\n\nBut there's also another behavior in GNU cp: `-f` also affects how cp deals with hard links. But that's for R2/R3 scope, not R1.\n\nAlso, GNU cp has a subtle interaction: if `-f` is combined with `-n`, GNU cp behavior is... let me check. Actually, looking at GNU cp's source, `-n` always takes precedence over `-f`. My implementation handles this correctly because the `-n` check happens in copyFile before doCopy is called.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the final files. The `stdinReader` global variable is fine for a CLI tool since tests run the binary as a separate process with a fresh global state for each case. I also need to make sure the `--` separator works correctly in parseFlags—after `--`, all subsequent arguments are treated as files rather than flags, which is important for filenames starting with `-`.\n\nOne concern with the doCopy function: I'm returning `out.Close()` but also have a deferred close, so the explicit close might return nil while the deferred close tries to close an already-closed file descriptor and returns an error.\n\nWriting the implementation... I'm realizing the requirements don't specify how -f and -i should interact when both are provided, so I'll keep it straightforward: treat each flag independently, with -n taking absolute precedence to prevent any overwrites. I've settled on the approach—time to implement it. For the test file, I'm setting up a normalizer that handles the binary name in the interactive prompt output, since my implementation hardcodes \"cp:\" while the reference binary prints \"gcp:\", and the normalizer will make them match for comparison purposes.\n\nThe interactive prompt will show \"cp: overwrite 'dest.txt'? \" in both cases after normalization, which should handle the core output. I'm checking GNU cp's actual format to make sure I'm matching the prompt structure correctly, including how it quotes the destination filename.\n\nNow I'm ready to start writing the implementation. One more edge case to verify: when testing the `-f` force flag with a read-only destination file, the Go binary should fail to open it for writing, remove the existing file (since the parent directory is writable), and then create a new one. Both approaches end up with the same result since the Go binary creates `readonly.txt` with mode 0o644, making it writable for the ref binary's subsequent copy operation. The test only checks file content, not permissions, so this works fine. Let me actually write the code now—though I'm second-guessing the force flag test logic and need to think through what happens when both binaries copy to the same destination. readonly.txt still has the new content after both operations succeed. Both binaries produce no output, so the test should pass. Though I'm realizing GNU cp might handle existing files differently than new ones—it could use different open flags depending on whether the destination already exists.\n\nI'm confident in the approach now, so let me implement both files. For the long flag handling, I need to make sure `applyLongFlag` properly processes `--force`, `--interactive`, and `--no-clobber`. Handling unknown flags...\n\nActually, I don't have test cases for unknown flags, so I'll skip that for now and focus on what the tests actually require—missing operand detection. I'm realizing there's a potential issue with `os.Stat` following symlinks—if dest is a symlink to a non-existent file, it'll fail and we'll treat it as non-existent, but if it points to an existing file, we'll proceed with the same-file and no-clobber checks. Since symlink handling is out of scope for R1, this behavior is acceptable. I should also make sure I'm handling the edge case where the source and destination are the same file.\n\nWriting the code...\n\nActually, I need to double-check my error message formatting. When `copyFile` returns an error, I'm including the user-provided path (which might be relative) along with the system error message. GNU cp outputs lowercase error messages, so I need to make sure mine matches that convention after normalizing the binary name. I'm checking whether `os.OpenFile` would return `ENOENT` when the parent directory doesn't exist—like trying to copy to a nonexistent subdirectory—and verifying that my error formatting matches gcp's output after case normalization.\n\nWriting the implementation... I'm realizing there's a buffering concern with `bufio.NewReader` consuming extra bytes ahead of what's needed, but since my tests provide exactly the input required and only read once, this shouldn't be an issue. For the interactive flag handling, I need to sort out the precedence: `-n` should skip without prompting, and `-f` should take priority in certain cases, so I need to think through the exact behavior before writing the actual code. The `-` argument gets treated as a regular filename rather than stdin, so if the file doesn't exist, it errors out just like GNU cp does. Now I'm walking through the test cases to make sure they won't interfere with each other when both binaries run—the first test copies src.txt to dest.txt, and the reference binary then overwrites it with the same content, which should match the expected output. Now checking the error case where multiple files are copied to a non-directory target, which should fail with an appropriate error message. Then moving into interactive mode tests where the `-i` flag prompts before overwriting—first with a yes response that allows the overwrite, then with a no response that skips it.\n\nNext I'm testing the force flag behavior with read-only files, where `-f` should remove the write protection and allow the overwrite to succeed. Then the no-clobber flag tests, where `-n` prevents overwriting existing files.\n\nFor the flag precedence test, when both `-n` and `-i` are specified, the no-clobber behavior takes priority and skips the file without prompting.\n\nNow I'm checking the error message output when gcp runs without arguments