flag from being triggered.\n\nNow I'm thinking about whether to add a SIGPIPE test similar to what the uniq tests have. Since the documentation requires all cmd utilities to install a SIGPIPE handler and the constitution mandates testing it, I should include one even though the core requirements only specify R1.1 through R1.4.\n\nActually, cp doesn't produce stdout output in the R1.1-R1.4 scope unless the verbose flag is used, which is part of R3.4. So triggering SIGPIPE would be difficult here. I'll skip the SIGPIPE test for now since there's no practical way to test it without the verbose flag, though the handler itself is still installed for safety.\n\nWriting test file... Writing test normalization functions... Writing test cases... Writing test cases... Writing test cases... Writing test cases... Writing test cases... Writing test cases...\n\nNow I'm closing out the test file and defining a helper function to write files to the temporary directory. Let me double-check the flag parsing logic for the combined `-ni` flags to make sure it correctly handles both the no-overwrite and interactive modes together. Now checking the \"no_args\" test case where cp runs with no arguments—my binary prints the error message and exits with code 1, matching gcp's output after normalization. For \"one_arg\" with just a source file, I need to verify it also produces the correct error.\n\nLet me trace through the interactive prompt handling to make sure the regex correctly normalizes both the Go binary and reference binary stderr outputs, stripping the binary name prefix so they match. Ref stderr: `gcp:` → `cp:`\n\nAfter promptNorm:\n- Go: `cp: overwrite 'dest.txt'? `\n- Ref: `cp: overwrite 'dest.txt'? `\n- Match ✓\n\nBut wait, there might be trailing content. The prompt doesn't end with \\n. So:\n- Go stderr raw: `cp: overwrite 'dest.txt'? ` (no trailing newline)\n- Ref stderr raw: `gcp: overwrite 'dest.txt'? ` (no trailing newline)\n\nThe regex `(?m)^` matches at the start of each line. With no newline, the entire string is one \"line\". The regex matches `cp:` at the beginning → replaces with `cp:` (no change for Go, `gcp:` → `cp:` for ref). After normalization both outputs match.\n\nNow I'm actually creating the test files. But first, let me verify the missing destination error message matches exactly. My output uses ASCII quotes in C locale, and gcp should too, so after normalization both should show `cp: missing destination file operand after 'src.txt'`. Looking at the GNU cp source code to understand the exact error message format. The code shows it uses `shell_escape_always_quoting_style` to quote the filename, which produces `'src.txt'` in the C locale, matching what I'd output. The `usage(EXIT_FAILURE)` call would print additional help text to stderr after the error message.\n\nAfter normalizing the output by removing the \"Try\" line and adjusting the binary name, both GNU cp and my implementation produce the same result. Now I'm checking an edge case where the destination is an existing directory—GNU cp should copy the source file into that directory with its original name.\n\nMy implementation handles this by checking if the destination path is a directory, and if so, appending the source filename to create the full destination path. This works whether the directory path has a trailing slash or not.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the actual files.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3401,"cache_read_input_tokens":34593,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3401},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"417d3d97-6d07-4097-ab1c-960277983888","uuid":"f3200146-70a9-41b6-830b-93a68a5e35ef"}
