g the prompt normalizer, since both binaries should prompt on stderr, read the \"n\" response from stdin, skip the overwrite, and exit with code 1.\n\nUpdating the test case... Adding test cases for flag precedence...\n\nNow I need to implement the core changes: switching from independent booleans to an ordered flag state system that tracks interactive mode and unlinkDest separately, fixing the error message format for the directory check, adding a sentinel error for when the user declines in interactive mode, and updating all the affected tests to match the new behavior.\n\nLet me also reconsider how `-f` and `-n` interact. Looking at GNU cp's implementation, `-n` only sets the interactive mode to always-no and doesn't touch unlinkDest, so the last flag wins for interactive mode while unlinkDest persists from `-f`. This means `-fn` results in skipping the file entirely since iAlwaysNo takes precedence, but `-nf` lets `-f` override it back to iAlwaysYes with unlinkDest still enabled.\n\nWriting the updated code... I'm realizing there's a subtle distinction in how GNU cp handles the `-f` flag—it sets the interactive mode to always yes, which means \"don't prompt, just overwrite,\" but I need to verify whether this is actually the default behavior or if `-f` adds something more specific like unlinking the destination file before opening it. Defining the interactive mode enum... Defining the flag handler...\n\nNow I'm walking through the test cases to verify the logic works correctly. With just `-f`, the interactive mode becomes default and unlinkDest is true, so when the destination exists and is read-only, it removes the file and retries the copy successfully. Without any flags, the default behavior allows overwriting existing writable files. I need to check what happens when the destination exists but is read-only with no flags specified.\n\nThe scenario where the destination is read-only and no flags are provided should fail because unlinkDest stays false, so the open fails and we don't retry. This matches the expected behavior of the standard cp command. Now I'm ready to implement the updated code, which includes adding a sentinel error for the declined case and using `errors.AsType` to check for it.\n\nWriting main.go... Continuing with flag parsing...\n\nNow I'm implementing the copy functions—first handling single file copies where if the destination is a directory, I append the source filename to it, then attempt the actual copy operation and return the appropriate exit code. For multiple source files, I'm validating that the destination is indeed a directory before iterating through each source.\n\nContinuing with the file copy logic... Continuing the file copy implementation...\n\nI'm finishing the error handling function that formats path errors appropriately, then checking the test results. I need to fix a few failing tests: the interactive overwrite prompt needs to return exit code 1, the flag parsing for `-in` needs adjustment, and the error message formatting should match expectations. Looking at the function sizes, most are within reasonable bounds, though I should verify copyMultiple doesn't exceed the limit.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the error message normalization for the multi_not_dir test case—I need to check whether the reference output already includes case normalization or if that's applied separately during comparison. Looking at the normalized error messages, my fix should produce the exact same output as the reference after all normalizations are applied. Now I need to verify the behavior for the multi-file case where the destination isn't a directory—I should check whether my implementation exits immediately with an error or attempts to process files first like GNU cp does.\n\nChecking the GNU cp source confirms it validates upfront that the target must be a directory when multiple sources are provided, then exits with code 1 if it's not. My implementation matches this behavior. I should also verify what exit code gcp uses when the user declines an overwrite prompt in interactive mode. I need to verify the actual behavior—different coreutils versions might handle declined overwrites differently, but the installed gcp clearly exits with code 1 when the user says no. I'll implement it the same way, using an error flag that produces the exit code without an extra error message, matching gcp's behavior exactly.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1974,"cache_read_input_tokens":90304,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1974},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"417d3d97-6d07-4097-ab1c-960277983888","uuid":"182fc390-b1af-4237-8345-a48e24a07901"}
